# grotto — a baroque nymphaeum cut into the terrace wall: a rusticated front of three arches, and
#          behind it a cave. Buontalenti at the Boboli, the shell grotto at Margate, Nymphenburg.
#
# THIS IS A COMPLETE CLIP AND NOT A FRAGMENT OF `clips/facility.clip`. It includes the facility's
# contract for the module and the materials and then declares its own `metre` and `bounds`, exactly
# as `clips/estate/BRIEF.md` says every building on this estate does. Open it on its own:
#
#     build\bin\WorldShaper.exe --clip-file clips\estate\grotto.clip
#
# WHERE IT STANDS ON THE ESTATE. North-west, cut into the terrace wall, at the site plan's x -34,
# z 20 relative to the facility, with its front facing -z (south) into the garden and the chambers
# running back into the hill (+z). It is built here in its OWN coordinates with its own origin on
# the centre line of the facade at the front face of the terrace wall, because a tight box round a
# building at the origin is cheaper than the same building sixty metres away with sixty metres of
# empty array in front of it. To stamp it into a world: translate by (-34, 0, 20).
#
# THE BOX, COUNTED RATHER THAN GUESSED. 22.0 x 8.0 x 14.0 m at the contract's metre 32 is
# 704 x 256 x 448 = 80,740,352 cells, five bytes a cell in the game = 404 MB. The brief allowed for
# 22 x 8 x 18 and 104 M cells; the last four metres were depth of solid hill BEHIND the chambers
# doing nothing but costing 23 M cells, so the hill is cut off at z = 12.0, which still leaves
# 3.45 m of rock behind the back wall of the hall. Everything else is as briefed.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT THIS PART PUTS UNDER LOAD
#
# 1. ONE OPENING, ONE ROOM, AND EVERYTHING ELSE ARRIVES BY BOUNCE — OFF WATER.
#    A 1.35 m oculus, twenty-two module-halves above the floor, over a 4.5 m basin of still black
#    water directly beneath it. The rotunda of the facility is that building's hardest indirect
#    case and it has an oculus AND sixteen drum windows; this has an oculus, six tapers, and a
#    doorway. The shaft lands on WATER, so what lights the vault of this room is the second bounce
#    off a specular surface — the term that fails first and fails as blotches — and after that off
#    a floor of loose rounded pebbles. The front arches are open to the garden, because a grotto
#    front must be; they light the first three metres and nothing past them. Everything from the
#    basin backwards — the cascade, the mask, the god, the whole encrusted vault — is lit by what
#    came off the water.
#
# 2. THE ROUGHEST SURFACE IN THE PROJECT, BY A LONG WAY — AND `roughness` IS THE WRONG NUMBER
#    FOR IT, WHICH IS THE FINDING AND NOT AN EXCUSE.
#    The shellwork is REAL MATTER and not a texture: a 45 mm skin lying in the stone behind every
#    interior surface, displaced outward into the room by two `cells` fields, so that shell,
#    pebble and stone alternate WITH the geometry rather than beside it.
#
#    The brief asked for `roughness` — faces per voxel — and said several times 1.0 would show it.
#    It does not, and the control says why. Measured at metre 32, `--part grotto_crust`, against
#    the SAME clip with the two displacements taken out so the crust is a plain 45 mm shell:
#
#                              volume        surface        roughness
#      encrusted             12.65 m3       765.4 m2          1.89
#      plain 45 mm shell     10.59 m3       665.4 m2          1.96
#
#    The encrustation makes the number go DOWN. `roughness` is surface over volume, and the
#    extreme of that ratio is a smooth sheet one voxel thick — so a thin shell scores high because
#    it is thin, and lumping it adds volume slightly faster than it adds face. The number is a
#    thinness meter, not a texture meter, and on `part_grotto` as a whole (nine hundred cubic
#    metres of solid hill) it reads 0.06 and means nothing at all.
#
#    WHAT DOES SHOW IT IS EXPOSED SURFACE AGAINST THE SMOOTH ROOM. `--part grot_hollow` at metre
#    32 — the air of the three chambers, so its surface IS the room's boundary as drawn — is
#    461.50 m3 of air inside 520.19 m2 of wall. The encrustation adds 100.0 m2 of exposed face to
#    that for 2.06 m3 of added stone: a fifth again as much surface as the whole room had, out of
#    two cubic metres of shells. A painted texture adds nought, which is the comparison that
#    matters and the one to make on any part that claims to be built rather than drawn.
#
# 3. CAUSTICS FROM A DAYLIGHT SHAFT ONTO A ROUGH RECEIVER THROUGH A SMALL APERTURE.
#    Sun through a 1.35 m hole, into `water` at ior 1.33 half a metre deep, onto a bowl lined with
#    displaced pebbles of granite, travertine, jasper and rosso. A caustic on a flat floor is the
#    easy version of this; the floor here is loose stone and the aperture is small.
#
# 4. TRANSLUCENT STONE WITH A LIGHT INSIDE IT.
#    `alabaster` is translucent=210 — stone you can see INTO — and the river god is 3.6 m of it
#    reclining on the cascade with an emissive core buried in his shoulder under 0.15 m of it.
#    `marble` at translucent=110 is half of this and nothing else in the project asks the rest.
#
# 5. THIN MATTER, in two kinds that break different things: seven 45 mm iron bars per side arch,
#    which is one and a half voxels at metre 32; and several hundred travertine stalactites 0.05
#    to 0.30 m long hanging off a doubly curved vault, which is thin matter that is not aligned
#    to anything.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# THE MODULE STILL GOVERNS THE FRONT, and the front only. The facade is architecture and belongs
# to the same estate as the orangery and the bell tower: it is 30 M wide and 16 M high, its centre
# arch is 8 M, its side arches 4 M, its piers 2 M and 5 M, its impost at 6 M and its cornice at
# 12 M. Every number in it is a multiple of M = 0.45. THE INSIDE IS A CAVE and is not on the
# module and is not meant to be — the two ellipsoids the vaults are struck from are chosen so the
# rooms clear the head heights measured below, and nothing else.
#
# THE SIGN OF A DISPLACEMENT, because it is the trap `documentation/20-clip-forge.md` §6 records
# and it is the mechanism this whole building is made of. `displace` ADDS to how far away the
# surface says it is, so a POSITIVE amount eats into the solid and a NEGATIVE one grows it. Every
# encrustation here is written `amount=-1.0` against a field that is already in metres, so the
# amount reads as "grow outward by whatever this field says", which is checkable in a slice and
# cannot be got backwards by accident.
#
# WHY THE LUMP FIELDS ARE MASKED BY A `smoothstep` OF THE SKIN, AND WHY THERE ARE TWO OF THEM. A
# `cells` site that happens to fall clear of the surface grows a blob of its own with nothing under
# it, and `components` finds it as a floating voxel: 18 components on a bare test sphere with no
# mask at all, 1 with one. The second mask is tighter and exists because a mask alone is not
# enough once a lump cone is steeper than forty-five degrees — the whole argument, with the three
# measurements that settle it, is at `grot_tight` below.
#
# WHAT IS UNIONED BACK AFTER THE CARVE, and it is the trap the manifest of the facility has a long
# comment about at its foot. The rooms are cut out of a hill. EVERYTHING THAT STANDS IN A ROOM —
# the encrustation, the stalactites, the pebbles, the cascade, the water, the god, the tapers, the
# grilles — is unioned after the difference, or the room eats it. The only things subtracted from
# the added layers again afterwards are the oculus and the three arch bores, because those must
# stay open and a 0.30 m stalactite growing out of the vault beside the oculus would otherwise be
# free to lean into it.

include "../facility/_contract.clip"

metre 32
bounds -11.0 -0.65 -1.5   11.0 7.35 12.5

# ================================================================================================
# THE FIELDS THE SURFACE IS MADE OF
# ================================================================================================
#
# A `cells` field answers with the distance in metres to the nearest of a scatter of sites, so it
# is zero at a site and about 0.8 of its `size` at the far corner between four. Remapped backwards
# it becomes a cone standing ON each site, in metres, which is exactly the argument `displace`
# wants: `amount=-1.0` then means "grow the surface outward by this many metres".
#
# THE SAME FIELDS ARE READ TWICE, once to move the stone and once to colour it, and that is the
# whole point of §6 of the forge document applied deliberately instead of as wear: the shell is
# not painted NEAR the lump, it is painted ON the lump, because both rules ask the same question
# of the same field at the same point.

let grot_shell_sites = cells size=0.200 seed=311
let grot_grit_sites  = cells size=0.075 seed=947
let grot_peb_sites   = cells size=0.090 seed=1153
let grot_stal_sites  = cells size=0.300 seed=613

# The stalactite field, stretched fourteen times along y so its cells are columns rather than
# blobs. `scale` divides the query point and multiplies the answer back by the SMALLEST factor,
# which is 1 here, so the value is untouched and only the shape of the cells changes. A columnar
# field displacing a down-facing vault grows a cone that hangs, and — because the field barely
# changes along y — the cone stays joined to the vault the whole way down instead of shedding its
# tip. That is why the stalactites come back as one component.
let grot_stal_col = scale { grot_stal_sites } x=1 y=14 z=1

# The broad grains that decide which encrustation goes where. Not `cells`: a patch of shellwork is
# metres across and irregular, and fbm is what irregular-at-a-metre looks like.
let grot_patch_a = fbm size=1.60 octaves=3 seed=77
let grot_patch_b = fbm size=1.10 octaves=3 seed=181
let grot_patch_c = fbm size=2.40 octaves=2 seed=233

# ================================================================================================
# THE HILL
# ================================================================================================
#
# A grotto is a room pretending to be a cave, and what makes it one rather than a shed is that
# there is a hill on top of it. This is that hill: one ellipsoid, cut off at the ground, at the
# terrace face in front and at z = 12.0 behind.
#
# Its summit is 7.20 (16 M) at z = 6.30, which is 1.13 m of cover over the crown of the hall's
# vault and enough for the oculus to be a hole through rock rather than a hole through a sheet.
# It falls to 3.21 at the outer edge of the facade and to nothing at x = +-11, so the facade
# stands proud of a bank rather than being pasted on a cliff — which is what "cut into the terrace
# wall" means and is the whole reason the front reads as a grotto and not as a garden building.
let grot_mound_body = ellipsoid 0 -1.35 6.30  rx=11.50 ry=8.55 rz=10.35
let grot_mound_cut  = box -11.0 -0.45 0.00   11.0 7.30 12.00
let grot_mound      = intersection { grot_mound_body grot_mound_cut }

# The garden apron in front of the terrace, so the front of the building has ground to stand on
# and the weathering has somewhere to run out to. It laps the mound by 0.05 in z.
let grot_apron = box -11.0 -0.45 -1.50   11.0 0.00 0.05

# ================================================================================================
# THE FACADE — 30 M wide, 16 M high, and the only part of this building on the module
# ================================================================================================
#
#   x  -6.75 .. -4.50   outer pier          5 M
#      -4.50 .. -2.70   side arch           4 M     springing 2.70 (6 M), crown 3.60 (8 M)
#      -2.70 .. -1.80   middle pier         2 M
#      -1.80 ..  1.80   centre arch         8 M     springing 3.15 (7 M), crown 4.95 (11 M)
#       1.80 ..  2.70   middle pier         2 M
#       2.70 ..  4.50   side arch           4 M
#       4.50 ..  6.75   outer pier          5 M
#
#   y   0.00 ..  5.40   the rusticated wall                  12 M
#       5.40 ..  5.85   the cornice, projecting 0.225         1 M
#       5.85 ..  6.30   the attic                             1 M
#       6.30 ..  7.20   the broken segmental pediment         2 M
#
# 1.35 (3 M) thick, from the front face at z = 0 to the front wall of the hall at z = 1.35.
let grot_fac_wall = box -6.75 -0.45 0.00   6.75 5.40 1.35  round=0.02

# THE IMPOST. A band right across the front at the springing of the side arches, 6 M up, standing
# 0.09 proud. It is what makes three arches of two different sizes read as one order.
let grot_fac_impost = box -6.80 2.700 -0.09   6.80 2.925 0.10

# THE CORNICE. A corona and a cyma over it. The moulding's two corners are back-and-bottom first
# and front-and-top second, and `run=x` puts the section in (z, y) — so the first corner is at the
# wall face and the second is 0.225 out in front of it and 0.45 higher.
let grot_fac_corona = box -6.975 5.400 -0.225   6.975 5.625 0.10
let grot_fac_cyma   = cyma -6.975 5.625 0.10   6.975 5.850 -0.225  run=x
let grot_fac_attic  = box -6.75 5.850 -0.05   6.75 6.300 1.35  round=0.02

# THE BROKEN SEGMENTAL PEDIMENT. An arc through (-2.70, 6.30), (0, 7.20) and (2.70, 6.30) is a
# circle of radius 4.50 (10 M) centred at (0, 2.70) — 1.8 R = 2.70^2/0.90 + 0.90, done once here
# so nobody does it again differently. The band is 0.45 (1 M) deep and the middle 3 M of it is
# taken out, which is what "broken" means.
let grot_ped_ring = difference {
    cylinder 0 2.70 0.675  r=4.500 h=1.05 axis=z
    cylinder 0 2.70 0.675  r=4.050 h=1.20 axis=z
}
let grot_ped_break = box -0.675 6.85 -0.40   0.675 7.40 1.60
let grot_pediment = difference {
    intersection { grot_ped_ring  box -2.75 6.25 -0.15   2.75 7.25 1.40 }
    grot_ped_break
}

# THE HERMS. Four terms of travertine, on the two middle piers and the two outer ones, standing
# from 3 M up to the cornice at 12 M: a plinth, a tapering shaft (a term tapers DOWNWARD, which is
# what tells it from a pilaster), shoulders, and a head. Built on +x and folded, so the two halves
# cannot drift apart. Each is bedded 0.15 into the wall face at z = 0.
let grot_herm_plinth = box -0.180 1.350 -0.240   0.180 1.620 0.15  round=0.012
let grot_herm_taper = union {
    box -0.135 1.570 -0.180   0.135 2.750 0.15  round=0.012
    box -0.180 2.650 -0.225   0.180 3.875 0.15  round=0.012
    box -0.225 3.775 -0.270   0.225 4.325 0.15  round=0.012
}
let grot_herm_shoulder = box -0.270 4.275 -0.300   0.270 4.550 0.15  round=0.02
let grot_herm_head     = ellipsoid 0 4.815 -0.105  rx=0.180 ry=0.360 rz=0.225
let grot_herm_cap      = box -0.225 5.130 -0.270   0.225 5.420 0.15  round=0.02
let grot_herm_one = union {
    grot_herm_plinth grot_herm_taper grot_herm_shoulder grot_herm_head grot_herm_cap
}
let grot_herms = intersection {
    mirror {
        union {
            translate { grot_herm_one } 2.250 0 0
            translate { grot_herm_one } 5.625 0 0
        }
    } axis=x
    box -6.10 1.30 -0.35   6.10 5.45 0.20
}

let grot_facade = union {
    grot_fac_wall grot_fac_impost grot_fac_corona grot_fac_cyma grot_fac_attic grot_pediment
}

# ================================================================================================
# THE ROOMS — the air, before anything is taken out of the hill
# ================================================================================================
#
# THE CENTRAL HALL. 9.00 x 7.20 m of floor — x -4.50 .. 4.50, z 1.35 .. 8.55 — with vertical walls
# to 2.70 and an irregular vault over them crowning at 6.00. The vault is an ellipsoid 4.95 x 3.30
# x 5.85 on the springing at 2.70, clipped to the room's own plan: rx is deliberately LARGER than
# the room's half width, so the vault meets the side walls at 4.07 and curves in from there
# instead of leaving a soffit ledge round the top of the wall.
#
# THE VAULT'S BOX RUNS FROM THE FLOOR AND NOT FROM THE SPRINGING, AND THAT IS THE ONE THING IN
# THIS FILE THAT MUST NOT BE TIDIED. The obvious way to write it is to clip the ellipsoid to
# y 2.65 .. 6.20, since below the springing it is inside the walls anyway and contributes nothing.
# It contributes nothing to the SHAPE. It contributes everything to the FIELD.
#
# A union answers with the smallest of its children's distances and an intersection with the
# largest. Clipped at 2.65, the vault says "+0.03, outside me" at a point 80 mm under the top of
# the wall box — and the wall box says "-0.08, just inside my top face", which is a face that is
# not a surface of anything, because the vault is above it. So the union answers -0.08 for a point
# that is a metre from any real boundary of the room, and every displacement grown off this field
# reads that as "the wall is 80 mm away" and grows a lump in mid air.
#
# MEASURED, at metre 8 with the weathering off, which is the control that settled it: 442
# components with the vault clipped at the springing and 3 with it clipped at the floor. The 439
# were single lumps of shellwork hanging 0.08 m under the springing line of the hall and 0.13 m
# under the springing of the chambers — which is exactly where the two wall boxes have their tops.
let grot_hall_walls = box -4.50 0.00 1.35   4.50 2.70 8.55
let grot_hall_dome  = ellipsoid 0 2.70 4.95  rx=4.95 ry=3.30 rz=5.85
let grot_hall_vault = intersection {
    grot_hall_dome
    box -4.50 0.00 1.35   4.50 6.20 8.55
}
let grot_hall_air = union { grot_hall_walls grot_hall_vault }

# THE TWO SIDE CHAMBERS. 3.60 x 4.50 each, x 5.40 .. 9.00 and z 2.25 .. 6.75, walls to 2.25 and a
# vault crowning at 4.50. Built on +x and folded.
let grot_ch_walls = box 5.40 0.00 2.25   9.00 2.25 6.75
let grot_ch_dome  = ellipsoid 7.20 2.25 4.50  rx=1.98 ry=2.25 rz=2.475
let grot_ch_vault = intersection {
    grot_ch_dome
    box 5.40 0.00 2.25   9.00 4.60 6.75
}
let grot_ch_one = union { grot_ch_walls grot_ch_vault }
let grot_chambers = intersection {
    mirror { grot_ch_one } axis=x
    box -9.05 -0.05 2.20   9.05 4.65 6.80
}

# THE BASIN. A bowl 4.5 m across (r = 2.25) and 0.55 deep sunk in the floor of the hall, centred
# on z = 5.40 so that it is directly under the oculus. Its lip is the floor: the water surface
# stands at y = 0.00 exactly, flush with the pavement, which is what makes it read as a still
# black mirror rather than as a puddle in a hole — and it is also what guarantees the water body
# touches the film coming down the cascade without a hairline anywhere.
let grot_bowl = cylinder 0 -0.275 5.40  r=2.25 h=0.56 axis=y

# What the crust and the pebbles are grown off. Deliberately NOT the arches, the passages or the
# oculus: those must stay clear, and a skin taken off them would encrust the one route through the
# building and the one hole the light comes in by.
let grot_hollow = union { grot_hall_air grot_chambers grot_bowl }

# THE THREE ARCHES, as bores through the facade. Each is a box to its springing and a cylinder on
# its axis for the head, run from 0.10 in front of the face to 0.10 past the back of it so no
# hairline of stone is left in either plane.
let grot_arch_c = union {
    box -1.80 -0.05 -0.10   1.80 3.15 1.45
    cylinder 0 3.15 0.675  r=1.80 h=1.55 axis=z
}
let grot_arch_s_one = union {
    box 2.70 -0.05 -0.10   4.50 2.70 1.45
    cylinder 3.60 2.70 0.675  r=0.90 h=1.55 axis=z
}
let grot_arch_s = intersection {
    mirror { grot_arch_s_one } axis=x
    box -4.55 -0.10 -0.15   4.55 3.65 1.50
}
let grot_arches = union { grot_arch_c grot_arch_s }

# THE TWO PASSAGES from the hall into the side chambers, through the 0.90 of rock at x 4.50..5.40.
# 1.35 (3 M) wide, round-headed, crown 3.15 — measured at 3.23 off `--slice z@4.3` at metre 8, and
# clear of the brief's 2.40 through a door by three quarters of a metre.
#
# HEAD HEIGHT IN THIS BUILDING IS MEASURED OFF SLICES AND NOT OFF `--gap y@`, AND THAT IS NOT A
# PREFERENCE. `gap_along` reports the run from the FIRST air cell in the column to the LAST, and
# this building has sky over it — so every vertical probe anywhere inside it comes back "8.000 m of
# air along y (BROKEN)", which is the height of the box. The facility can use `--gap y@` because
# the facility has a roof. A `--slice z@` at metre 8 gives the whole profile across x at 0.125 m
# instead, which is more readings for one sample, and the numbers are in the ledger at the foot of
# this file.
#
# (And `--gap y@a,b` has its arguments crossed in `tools/clipcheck.cpp` besides: for the y axis it
# scales `a` by the z origin and then uses it as an x index. On a clip whose bounds do not start at
# the same number on both axes — this one starts at x -11 and z -1.5 — `y@0,3` probes x -9.5,
# z 12.5. It is written up in grotto.md; nothing here works around it, because slices are better.)
let grot_pass_one = union {
    box 4.35 -0.05 3.60   5.55 2.475 4.95
    cylinder 4.95 2.475 4.275  r=0.675 h=1.30 axis=x
}
let grot_passes = intersection {
    mirror { grot_pass_one } axis=x
    box -5.60 -0.10 3.55   5.60 3.20 5.00
}

# THE OCULUS. 1.35 m (3 M) across, one of them, bored straight up out of the crown of the hall's
# vault at x = 0, z = 5.40 — over the middle of the basin — and out through 1.13 m of hill to the
# sky. The vault at that axis stands at 5.99 and the hill at 7.13, so this is a light WELL with a
# metre and an eighth of rock in it and not a hole in a sheet, which is the whole reason the shaft
# has an edge. NO SPLAY: a cone under it was drawn first and does nothing, because everything it
# would have widened is already air.
let grot_oculus = cylinder 0 6.30 5.40  r=0.675 h=2.60 axis=y

let grot_void = union { grot_hollow grot_arches grot_passes grot_oculus }

# What must stay clear whatever is added back afterwards.
let grot_keep_clear = union { grot_arches grot_passes grot_oculus }

# ================================================================================================
# THE ENCRUSTATION — real matter, not a texture
# ================================================================================================
#
# A skin 45 mm thick lying in the STONE just behind every interior surface. It is already part of
# the hill, so unioning it back changes not one voxel — until it is displaced, and then every
# millimetre it moves is stone that was not there before, growing INTO the room.
#
# `by=-0.045` AND NOT `by=0.045`. `offset` ADDS its number to the distance, so a POSITIVE value
# shrinks a shape and a negative one grows it — the same sign convention as `displace` and the
# opposite of what the name suggests. Written the obvious way round, `offset { hollow } by=0.045`
# is the room made SMALLER, the difference with the room itself is the empty set, and what came
# out was a crust grown off a surface 45 mm inside the room with nothing joining it to the hill.
# It measured plausibly and it was wrong. The same slip is in the water film and the grilles below
# and all three are written negative here.
let grot_skin = difference { offset { grot_hollow } by=-0.045   grot_hollow }

# Inside-ness of that skin, which every lump field below is multiplied by. Without it a site that
# lands clear of the surface grows a bead of its own in mid air.
#
# TWO MASKS, AND THE TIGHT ONE IS THE WHOLE OF WHY THIS BUILDING IS ONE COMPONENT.
#
# A displaced skin is solid wherever `lump(p) > distance(p)`. Take one `cells` site sitting a
# height h clear of the surface, carrying a cone A tall and R across. At the site itself the test
# is A > h. Half way back down to the surface it is A(1 - (h/2)/R) > h/2. If the cone is STEEPER
# than forty-five degrees — A > R — the second can fail while the first holds, and what you get is
# a bead of shellwork hanging in the air with a gap under it. Work it through and the cone is
# joined to its own surface for every site iff **A <= R**, or else iff the mask is already nought
# by h = R·A/(A-R).
#
# The coarse lump is 0.12 tall on a 0.06 radius, which is a cone at sixty-three degrees, because a
# shell set in mortar is a steep thing and flattening it to forty-five costs a fifth of the
# roughness. Measured on a test sphere at metre 32, one displaced skin, nothing else:
#
#   A 0.12 / R 0.06, loose mask     roughness 1.85     72 components, 154 floating
#   A 0.12 / R 0.12, loose mask     roughness 1.49      3 components,   2 floating
#   A 0.12 / R 0.06, TIGHT mask     roughness 1.85      1 component,    0 floating
#
# So the cone keeps its shape and the mask is cut off at 0.058 — just under the 0.06 at which a
# 0.12/0.06 cone can strand itself. The stalactites keep the loose mask and are safe on it for a
# different reason: their field is columnar, so walking back up towards the vault does not move
# you out of the cone at all. The pebbles keep it because 0.036 is small enough that the loose
# mask has already fallen below h by the time h reaches 0.03.
let grot_near  = smoothstep { grot_skin } from=0.40  to=-0.10
let grot_tight = smoothstep { grot_skin } from=0.058 to=-0.02

# THE SHELLWORK, on everything above the dado at 3 M. Two scales: 0.20 m sites carrying a 0.12 m
# cone, which is the shell and the pebble, and 0.075 m sites carrying 0.045, which is the grit and
# the mortar between them. `max` and not `add`, so the fine scale roughens the coarse one instead
# of inflating it — added, the two fill each other's hollows and the surface comes back smooth and
# fat, which was the first pass and measured 1.11 where this measures 2.
let grot_lump_c = remap { grot_shell_sites } from=0.0 to=0.060 low=0.120 high=0.0
let grot_lump_f = remap { grot_grit_sites }  from=0.0 to=0.024 low=0.045 high=0.0
let grot_lump_h = remap { axis of=y }        from=1.15 to=1.55 low=0.0   high=1.0
let grot_lump   = multiply { max { grot_lump_c grot_lump_f }  grot_lump_h  grot_tight }

# THE STALACTITES. The columnar field again, a cone 0.15 m across standing 0.30 m tall on every
# site, scaled by height so that the ones near the crown at 6.00 are the full 0.30 and the ones at
# the haunch are 0.05. Several hundred of them: the vault above 4.35 is about forty square metres
# and the sites are 0.30 apart.
let grot_stal_cone = remap { grot_stal_col } from=0.0 to=0.075 low=0.300 high=0.0
let grot_stal_high = remap { axis of=y }     from=4.35 to=5.70 low=0.02  high=1.0
let grot_stal_amt  = multiply { grot_stal_cone grot_stal_high grot_near }

# THE PEBBLED FLOOR. 0.09 m sites carrying 0.036, over the pavement and down the whole of the
# basin's bowl — which is the point of taking it below the water line as well as above it, because
# the receiver the caustic lands on is the bottom of the basin and not the floor round it.
let grot_peb_cone = remap { grot_peb_sites } from=0.0 to=0.030 low=0.036 high=0.0
let grot_peb_low  = remap { axis of=y }      from=0.30 to=0.10 low=0.0   high=1.0
let grot_peb_amt  = multiply { grot_peb_cone grot_peb_low grot_near }

# And the two skins, each cut to where its own displacement belongs, then grown.
let grot_skin_up = intersection { grot_skin  box -9.30 1.30 1.25   9.30 6.30 8.65 }
let grot_skin_fl = intersection { grot_skin  box -9.30 -0.70 1.25   9.30 0.12 8.65 }

# TWO DISPLACEMENTS ON ONE SKIN AND NOT TWO SKINS: the shellwork first, the stalactites on top of
# what the shellwork made. `skip_slack()` sums the reach of EVERY displace node in the whole field
# whether it is used or not, so nesting saves nothing there — but it saves a second shell, and a
# stalactite that grows off an already-encrusted vault is joined to it by construction.
#
# WHAT THIS BUILDING COSTS AND WHERE THE COST IS, because at metre 32 it is the headline number and
# it is worth being able to point at the term responsible. The sampler may only skip ahead by
# `distance - skip_slack`, and `skip_slack` is TWICE the summed reach of every displacement:
#
#   the shellwork lumps        0.120      the encrustation
#   the stalactites            0.300      <- 58% of the whole budget, in one term
#   the pebbles                0.036
#   weather overgrown          0.022
#   weather cracks             0.027
#   weather sea                0.014
#   ----------------------------------
#   sum                        0.519  ->  skip_slack 1.039 m
#
# A metre of slack in a 22 x 8 x 14 box that is two fifths solid rock means essentially nothing is
# ever skipped: every one of the eighty million cells is evaluated. That is the price of building
# the encrustation as matter rather than as a texture, it is bought deliberately, and the one dial
# that would halve it is the stalactites' 0.30 — which is the length the brief asked for.
let grot_crust_a = displace { grot_skin_up grot_lump }     amount=-1.0
let grotto_crust = displace { grot_crust_a grot_stal_amt } amount=-1.0
let grotto_pebbles = displace { grot_skin_fl grot_peb_amt } amount=-1.0

# ================================================================================================
# THE SPRING — a mask, a fall, a cascade, and the basin
# ================================================================================================
#
# THE MASK. A face of travertine on the back wall of the hall at 7 M, with the water coming out of
# its mouth. Two eyes and a mouth taken out of one ellipsoid and a beard under it: it does not
# have to be good sculpture, it has to be a thing with a hole in it that reads as a head.
let grot_mask_face  = ellipsoid 0 3.30 8.50  rx=0.90 ry=0.78 rz=0.50
let grot_mask_beard = ellipsoid 0 2.70 8.52  rx=0.62 ry=0.55 rz=0.42
let grot_mask_brow  = box -0.92 3.55 8.00   0.92 3.74 8.60  round=0.06
let grot_mask_eye_l = sphere -0.34 3.38 8.16  r=0.15
let grot_mask_eye_r = sphere  0.34 3.38 8.16  r=0.15
let grot_mask_mouth = box -0.24 2.90 8.02   0.24 3.10 8.62  round=0.05
let grot_mask = difference {
    union { grot_mask_face grot_mask_beard grot_mask_brow }
    grot_mask_eye_l grot_mask_eye_r grot_mask_mouth
}

# THE CASCADE. Four steps of 0.45 rise and 0.225 run, from 1.80 at the back wall down to the lip
# of the basin at z = 7.65. NOT A STAIR and not written as one: nobody climbs it, and the brief's
# 0.18 and 0.32 are for treads a person walks on.
let grot_casc = union {
    box -1.80 -0.05 8.325   1.80 1.80 8.60
    box -1.80 -0.05 8.100   1.80 1.35 8.330
    box -1.80 -0.05 7.875   1.80 0.90 8.105
    box -1.80 -0.05 7.650   1.80 0.45 7.880
}

# THE WATER, in four bodies that all touch each other.
#
#   the basin      r 2.25, half a metre deep, its surface at 0.00 — the floor's own level
#   the film       a 50 mm shell over the cascade, which is what a sheet of water running over
#                  four steps actually is, and two voxels thick at the contract's metre
#   the fall       from the mask's mouth at 3.00 down to the top step at 1.80
#   the spill      the short body over the lip that laps the film and the basin together, because
#                  two shapes that meet exactly at a plane do not necessarily share a voxel
# AND THE PEBBLES ARE PAINTED AFTER IT, which is why the paint stack below ends water, THEN
# pebbles, and not the other way about. Water has to go on late, because the film lies over stone
# that is itself painted late; but painted over an unbroken cylinder it also covers every pebble
# in the bottom of the basin — and the bottom of the basin is the receiver the caustic lands on
# and the whole reason the pebbles go below the water line as well as above it. One rule after the
# other costs nothing; the first version of this subtracted the pebble layer from the water body
# instead and doubled the sample time, because it put a displacement inside a shape that had none.
let grot_water_basin = cylinder 0 -0.275 5.40  r=2.250 h=0.55 axis=y
let grot_water_film  = intersection {
    difference { offset { grot_casc } by=-0.05   grot_casc }
    box -1.35 -0.10 7.55   1.35 1.95 8.60
}
let grot_water_fall  = box -0.28 1.28 8.12   0.28 3.06 8.30
let grot_water_spill = box -0.62 -0.10 7.50   0.62 0.14 7.82
let grotto_water = union {
    grot_water_basin grot_water_film grot_water_fall grot_water_spill
}

# ================================================================================================
# THE RIVER GOD — 3.6 m of alabaster with a light inside his shoulder
# ================================================================================================
#
# A large irregular translucent mass, reclining on the cascade, spanning x -1.88 .. 1.78. The
# drapery under him is a slab bedded 0.12 into the third step, so the contact with the rock is a
# surface and not a tangent; every other piece overlaps the one before it by at least 0.05, which
# is what keeps 3.6 m of loose anatomy at one component.
let grot_god_drape = box -1.85 0.78 7.86   1.85 1.10 8.32  round=0.09
let grot_god_hips  = ellipsoid -0.75 1.40 8.09  rx=0.65 ry=0.42 rz=0.38
let grot_god_torso = ellipsoid  0.10 1.45 8.09  rx=0.78 ry=0.42 rz=0.38
let grot_god_chest = ellipsoid  0.95 1.60 8.06  rx=0.60 ry=0.48 rz=0.40
let grot_god_neck  = capsule 1.30 1.82 8.06   1.52 1.96 8.06  r=0.17
let grot_god_head  = sphere 1.60 2.04 8.06  r=0.28
let grot_god_legs  = capsule -1.15 1.30 8.09  -1.60 1.05 8.25  r=0.28
let grot_god_arm   = capsule  1.42 1.82 7.84   0.46 1.24 7.80  r=0.17
let grot_god_urn   = ellipsoid 0.30 1.06 7.78  rx=0.30 ry=0.30 rz=0.27
let grotto_god = union {
    grot_god_drape grot_god_hips grot_god_torso grot_god_chest
    grot_god_neck grot_god_head grot_god_legs grot_god_arm grot_god_urn
}

# THE LIGHT INSIDE HIM. Buried in the chest with 0.15 m of alabaster over it on every side, so
# nothing of it is visible as a surface and the only way it can reach the room is THROUGH the
# stone. That is the subsurface case, and it is why this figure is alabaster and not marble.
let grotto_god_lamp = ellipsoid 0.95 1.60 8.14  rx=0.28 ry=0.24 rz=0.20

# ================================================================================================
# THE IRON GRILLES — seven bars of 45 mm to a side arch
# ================================================================================================
#
# `repeat` at 0.225 with nx=3 gives seven bars either side of the arch axis at x = 3.60, and the
# 0.045 bar fits comfortably inside its own 0.225 period, which is the condition `repeat` needs
# before it can say anything honest about distance.
#
# The whole grille is then cut to the arch bore GROWN by 0.06, so every bar runs 60 mm into the
# jamb, the sill and the arch ring rather than butting them — which is the difference between one
# component and fifteen.
let grot_bar_one   = box 3.5775 -0.10 0.6175   3.6225 3.70 0.6625
let grot_bar_fan   = repeat { grot_bar_one } x=0.225 nx=3
let grot_rail_low  = box 2.60 0.855 0.6125   4.60 0.900 0.6675
let grot_rail_high = box 2.60 1.980 0.6125   4.60 2.025 0.6675
let grot_grille_one = intersection {
    union { grot_bar_fan grot_rail_low grot_rail_high }
    offset { grot_arch_s_one } by=-0.06
}
let grotto_grilles = intersection {
    mirror { grot_grille_one } axis=x
    box -4.65 -0.15 0.55   4.65 3.70 0.72
}

# ================================================================================================
# THE SIX TAPERS — three to a side chamber, and nothing else in them
# ================================================================================================
#
# On the outer wall of each chamber at x = 9.00, at 4 M above the floor. A backplate bedded 20 mm
# into the wall, an arm, a bowl, a taper of `wax` and a flame of `taper`. Built on +x and folded,
# which is also what makes them six rather than three.
let grot_tp_plate = box 8.855 1.62 -0.14   9.080 2.02 0.14  round=0.02
let grot_tp_arm   = capsule 8.95 1.80 0   8.62 1.99 0  r=0.055
let grot_tp_bowl  = cylinder 8.62 2.015 0  r=0.110 h=0.075 axis=y
let grot_tp_wax   = cylinder 8.62 2.145 0  r=0.035 h=0.230 axis=y
let grot_tp_flame = ellipsoid 8.62 2.295 0  rx=0.034 ry=0.068 rz=0.034
let grot_tp_metal = union { grot_tp_plate grot_tp_arm grot_tp_bowl }

let grot_brackets_e = union {
    translate { grot_tp_metal } 0 0 3.15
    translate { grot_tp_metal } 0 0 4.50
    translate { grot_tp_metal } 0 0 5.85
}
let grot_wax_e = union {
    translate { grot_tp_wax } 0 0 3.15
    translate { grot_tp_wax } 0 0 4.50
    translate { grot_tp_wax } 0 0 5.85
}
let grot_flames_e = union {
    translate { grot_tp_flame } 0 0 3.15
    translate { grot_tp_flame } 0 0 4.50
    translate { grot_tp_flame } 0 0 5.85
}
let grotto_brackets = intersection {
    mirror { grot_brackets_e } axis=x
    box -9.15 1.55 2.95   9.15 2.10 6.05
}
let grotto_tapers = intersection {
    mirror { grot_wax_e } axis=x
    box -9.05 2.02 3.05   9.05 2.27 5.95
}
let grotto_flames = intersection {
    mirror { grot_flames_e } axis=x
    box -9.05 2.22 3.05   9.05 2.38 5.95
}

# ================================================================================================
# ASSEMBLY
# ================================================================================================
#
# The mass, then the carve, and then everything that stands in what was carved — with the oculus
# and the three arch bores taken back out of the added layers, so that no stalactite and no lump
# of shellwork can close the one hole the light comes in by or the one way in.
let grot_mass = union { grot_mound grot_apron grot_facade grot_herms }

let grot_carved = difference { grot_mass  grot_void }

let grot_added = difference {
    union { grotto_crust grotto_pebbles grot_casc grot_mask }
    grot_keep_clear
}

let part_grotto = union {
    grot_carved
    grot_added
    grotto_god
    grotto_god_lamp
    grotto_water
    grotto_grilles
    grotto_brackets
    grotto_tapers
    grotto_flames
}

# ================================================================================================
# PAINT
# ================================================================================================
#
# Every rule keys on a shape bound in THIS file and every one tests `below=0.02` and never
# `below=0`, so that a voxel carried outside its own shape by a weathering deformation is still
# caught by the rule that owns it instead of falling back to the coat underneath.
#
# HOW A PATTERN BECOMES A SHAPE, because half the rules below depend on it and it is not obvious.
# `paint <material> where=<pattern> on=<shape>` exists, but `on=` is only a BOUNDING BOX — it does
# not mask the rule to the shape — so on a building where the box round the encrustation is the
# box round the whole room it would paint the floor and the grilles as well. Instead every zone
# here is written as an INTERSECTION of one of my own shapes with a remapped field:
#
#     remap { field } from=hi to=lo low=-0.05 high=0.05
#
# is negative where the field is above the threshold and positive where it is below, which is
# exactly what "inside" means to everything else in this language. Intersected with a real shape
# it is a real shape, with a real bounding box, that the sampler can settle for a whole block.
#
# The order is the order a grotto is built in: the rock, the turf on it, the front, the floor, the
# encrustation, and last the things standing in the room.

paint tuff where=part_grotto below=0.02

# THE TURF. Up-facing surfaces of the mound only, and the zone deliberately excludes a box round
# the rooms — otherwise the rule reaches the floor of the hall, which is inside the hill, faces
# up, and would come back as lawn.
let grot_turf_zone = difference {
    grot_mound
    box -9.60 -0.70 1.20   9.60 2.70 8.70
}
paint lawn where=grot_turf_zone below=0.02 facing=y at=0.55
paint gravel where=grot_apron below=0.02 facing=y at=0.55

# THE FRONT. Granite ground and tuff courses over it: the courses are a `stripes` field at 0.90
# (2 M) turned into a shape and intersected with the facade, so the rustication is a real banding
# of two stones and not a wash.
let grot_course_band = remap { stripes axis=y period=0.90 duty=0.5 } from=0.55 to=0.45 low=-0.05 high=0.05
let grot_fac_courses = intersection { grot_facade grot_course_band }
paint granite where=grot_facade below=0.02
paint tuff    where=grot_fac_courses below=0.02
paint travertine where=grot_herms below=0.02

# THE ENCRUSTATION. Tuff is the ground it is set into; the shell, the faience, the porcelain and
# the two coloured stones are set ON the lumps, keyed on the SAME `cells` fields that made them.
# That is the whole of what §6 of the forge document means by weathering following the shape, done
# deliberately rather than as wear: the shell is not near the lump, it IS the lump.
let grot_lump_top  = remap { grot_shell_sites } from=0.055 to=0.030 low=0.05 high=-0.05
let grot_grit_top  = remap { grot_grit_sites }  from=0.022 to=0.012 low=0.05 high=-0.05
let grot_enc_shell = intersection { grotto_crust grot_lump_top }
let grot_enc_grit  = intersection { grotto_crust grot_grit_top }
let grot_enc_faie  = intersection { grot_enc_shell  remap { grot_patch_a } from=0.28 to=0.14 low=-0.05 high=0.05 }
let grot_enc_porc  = intersection { grot_enc_shell  remap { grot_patch_b } from=0.32 to=0.18 low=-0.05 high=0.05 }
let grot_enc_mala  = intersection { grot_enc_grit   remap { grot_patch_c } from=0.30 to=0.16 low=-0.05 high=0.05 }
let grot_enc_onyx  = intersection { grot_enc_grit   remap { grot_patch_a } from=-0.18 to=-0.32 low=-0.05 high=0.05 }

paint tuff      where=grotto_crust below=0.02
paint shellwork where=grot_enc_shell below=0.02
paint faience   where=grot_enc_faie below=0.02
paint porcelain where=grot_enc_porc below=0.02
paint malachite where=grot_enc_mala below=0.02
paint onyx      where=grot_enc_onyx below=0.02

# THE STALACTITES, keyed on the columnar field that grew them and cut to the vault above 4.20 so
# the rule cannot reach the haunch of the room where the same field is only moving the surface by
# a few millimetres.
let grot_stal_zone = intersection {
    grotto_crust
    remap { grot_stal_col } from=0.060 to=0.030 low=0.05 high=-0.05
    box -4.55 4.20 1.30   4.55 6.25 8.60
}
paint travertine where=grot_stal_zone below=0.02

# WHAT STANDS IN THE ROOM. Last, so nothing above can reach it.
paint travertine where=grot_mask below=0.02
paint tuff       where=grot_casc below=0.02
paint alabaster  where=grotto_god below=0.02
paint lamp       where=grotto_god_lamp below=0.02
paint iron       where=grotto_grilles below=0.02
paint bronze     where=grotto_brackets below=0.02
paint wax        where=grotto_tapers below=0.02
paint taper      where=grotto_flames below=0.02
paint water      where=grotto_water below=0.02

# THE PEBBLED FLOOR, AND IT IS LAST FOR A REASON. Four stones, each keyed on a broad grain so the
# pebbles come in drifts rather than in a salt-and-pepper that averages to grey at any distance;
# every rule is intersected with the pebble layer itself, so none of them can reach a wall. They go
# on AFTER the water, so that the pebbles in the bottom of the basin are pebbles and not a flat
# sheet of blue — which is the difference between a caustic with a rough receiver and a caustic on
# a mirror, and the rough one is the case this building was asked for.
let grot_peb_jasper = intersection { grotto_pebbles  remap { grot_patch_a } from=0.30 to=0.16 low=-0.05 high=0.05 }
let grot_peb_rosso  = intersection { grotto_pebbles  remap { grot_patch_b } from=0.34 to=0.20 low=-0.05 high=0.05 }
let grot_peb_trav   = intersection { grotto_pebbles  remap { grot_patch_c } from=-0.16 to=-0.30 low=-0.05 high=0.05 }
paint granite    where=grotto_pebbles below=0.02
paint travertine where=grot_peb_trav  below=0.02
paint jasper     where=grot_peb_jasper below=0.02
paint rosso      where=grot_peb_rosso below=0.02

# ================================================================================================
# WEATHERING — the main tool here, not a finishing touch
# ================================================================================================
#
# EVERY ONE NAMES ONE OF MY OWN SHAPES. Without `on=` a weathering acts on the whole clip: it
# would bleach the lawn on top of the hill, scour the herms and sand the water, because the coats
# it adds are keyed on a value rather than on a place and they go on after everything painted
# above. That is why the facility shipped with none at all for its whole first life.
#
#   overgrown   on the encrustation, which is the wet surface: a grotto vault drips, and what
#               grows on it grows in the hollows between the lumps, which is where `occlusion`
#               already says the damp is
#   sea         round the basin, and `level=` is the tide line, so it is set to the water surface
#               at 0.00 — salt bleaching above it, barnacle standing proud below it
#   cracks      on the facade, which is the one part of this building that was ever built and is
#               therefore the one part that can be seen to have failed
# AND THE CRACKS ARE SCOPED ON THE FACADE **AS CARVED**, WHICH IS NOT THE SAME SHAPE AS THE
# FACADE. `weather`'s scope mask is one everywhere INSIDE the named shape, and `grot_facade` is
# the block before its three arches are bored out of it — so the iron grilles, which stand in the
# middle of the side arches and are therefore inside that block, were being cracked as if they
# were masonry. Measured on the facade alone at metre 32: 82 components with the uncarved scope
# and 1 with this one, and every one of the eighty-one was a piece of a 45 mm bar with a 29 mm
# fissure bitten out of it.
let grot_fac_weathered = difference { grot_facade grot_arches }

weather overgrown 0.55 scale=0.9 seed=71 on=grotto_crust
weather sea 0.40 scale=0.7 seed=137 level=0.00 on=grotto_pebbles
weather cracks 0.25 scale=1.2 seed=29 on=grot_fac_weathered

variation colour=0.045 rough=0.090 seed=61

solid part_grotto

# ================================================================================================
# WHAT IT MEASURES — the ledger, at the contract's metre 32 unless said otherwise
# ================================================================================================
#
# THE BOX. 704 x 256 x 448 = 80,740,352 cells, 22.0 x 8.0 x 14.0 m, 404 MB in the game at five
# bytes a cell.
#
# THE WHOLE PART, `clipcheck clips/estate/grotto.clip --metre 32`:
#
#   volume        31,069,650 voxels   948.17 m3   38.48% of the box
#   surface       1,760,108 faces     1718.86 m2
#   roughness     0.06 faces per voxel   — a hill; see the note in the load list above
#   materials     24 distinct, and NO rule that never fired
#   components    87, largest 31,069,550, one hundred floating voxels
#   worldbox      -11.000 -0.656 -1.500   11.000 7.188 12.000 m
#   peak memory   1,135,108 kB = 1.11 GB
#   wall clock    2,543 s = 42 min 23 s
#
# THE WALL CLOCK IS NOT A FIGURE ANYBODY SHOULD COMPARE ANYTHING TO. Thirteen other agents were
# building on this machine while it ran, at a load average of eighteen, and the same file at metre
# 16 took 97 s early in the session and 296 s late in it for identical output. What IS comparable
# is the memory, which is a function of the box, and the arithmetic of `skip_slack` written out at
# the encrustation above: 1.039 m of slack in a box two fifths full of rock means no cell is ever
# skipped, and eighty million field evaluations is what this building costs by construction.
#
# EIGHTY-SEVEN COMPONENTS AND A HUNDRED FLOATING VOXELS, AND WHAT THEY ARE. One to three voxels
# each, all of them on the two faces of the facade at z = -0.12 and z = 1.34, and they are chips
# knocked off by `weather cracks`. Measured on a bare block with the same crack setting and nothing
# else in the clip: 15 islands of 15 voxels. It is what that weathering does — it opens a fissure
# 29 mm deep and sometimes the fissure meets itself round a corner. `despeckle` does not remove
# them because `despeckle` repaints colour specks and does not delete matter. The number that was
# worth chasing was the 850 components the same file reported before the two connectivity fixes
# above (the false union face, and the 63-degree lump cone), and those are gone.
#
#   metre 16      3,885,885 voxels, 51 components, 58 floating, 54.7 MB, 296 s
#   metre  8        495,646 voxels, 12 components, 12 floating
#
# HEAD HEIGHT, and it is measured off `--slice z@` at metre 8 and not off `--gap y@` — see the note
# at the passages for why a building with sky over it cannot be probed with `--gap` along y. Every
# figure below is the clear height above the floor at that (x, z), to the 0.125 m of a metre-8
# voxel, and the encrustation and the stalactites are IN it:
#
#   z = 1.60  hall, just inside the front wall   2.85 at x -4.4 (hard against the wall)
#                                                5.47 on the centre line
#   z = 2.50  hall                               3.35 at x -4.4 ... 5.85 at x 0
#             side chambers                      2.35 at x +-8.9 and +-5.6 ... 3.73 at x +-7.1
